  "generated_text": "1. We need to find the probability of getting exactly 2 tails in 7 flips of an unfair coin.\n2. This is a binomial probability problem where \\( n = 7 \\) (number of flips), \\( k = 2 \\) (number of tails), and \\( p = \\frac{3}{4} \\) (probability of tails).\n3. The binomial probability formula is: \\( P(X = k) = \\binom{n}{k} p^k (1-p)^{n-k} \\).\n4. Here, \\( \\binom{n}{k} \\) is the binomial coefficient, calculated as \\( \\binom{n}{k} = \\frac{n!}{k!(n-k)!} \\).\n5. Plugging in the values, we get:\n   \\[\n   P(X = 2) = \\binom{7}{2} \\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)^2 \\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)^5\n   \\]\n6. Simplify the expression and calculate.\n\nSo, the probability of getting exactly 2 tails in 7 flips is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{189}{1024}}\n\\]",
  "grade": {
    "error": null,
    "extracted_answer": "[189/1024, '\\\\frac{189}{1024}']",
    "gold_answer": "The probability that two coins land tails and five coins land heads, in some order, is $\\left( \\frac{3}{4} \\right)^2 \\left( \\frac{1}{4} \\right)^5=\\frac{9}{16384}$. Any two of the seven coins can be tails, so this can happen in $\\binom{7}{2}=21$ ways. Therefore, the probability that exactly two coins come up tails is $21 \\cdot \\frac{9}{16384}=\\boxed{\\frac{189}{16384}}$.",
    "is_correct": false
  },
